# core/lifecycle - What state are elements in?
#
# Answers: elements carrying an element-level lifecycle status, grouped with their
# label and model.
#
# Does NOT prove: coverage. Lifecycle is sparse and notation-specific, so the
# elements NOT listed here are overwhelmingly ones nobody recorded a status for,
# not ones in a healthy state.
#
# REQUIRES capabilities.element_lifecycle. There is no core lifecycle predicate:
# Backstage spells it bs:lifecycleState, LeanIX lmm:factSheetStatus, and ArchiMate
# output carries none at all. The profile binds whichever apply as a chain, and
# {{ROLES:element_lifecycle}} covers a mixed dataset.
#
# Do not confuse this with the model-level adms:status in the provenance graph,
# which describes the conversion, not the architecture. "Completed" there means the
# converter finished.
#
# The model comes from {{MEMBERSHIP:element}} rather than a hardcoded dct:isPartOf
# hop, which was the folder edge rather than model membership and so reached the
# model for some notations only. It matters more here than elsewhere: lifecycle is
# notation-specific, so this template's rows are concentrated in exactly the
# notations whose folder chain differs.
#
# Parameters: LIMIT
{{PREFIXES}}
SELECT ?element ?label ?statusPredicate ?status ?model
WHERE {
  {{GRAPH_OPEN:semantic}}
    VALUES ?statusPredicate { {{ROLES:element_lifecycle}} }
    ?element ?statusPredicate ?status .
    OPTIONAL { ?element {{PATH:label}} ?label }
    OPTIONAL { {{MEMBERSHIP:element}} }
  {{GRAPH_CLOSE}}
}
ORDER BY ?status ?label ?element
LIMIT {{LIMIT}}
